  <subsection xml:id="course-desc">
    <title>Course Description</title>
    <p>
      In this introduction to statistical methodology, students identify methods of acquiring, organizing and analyzing data so as to make decisions and respond to arguments. Topics include graphical representation of data, probability distributions, confidence intervals and hypothesis test, parametric and non-parametric techniques, regression and correlation, and analysis of variance. Special emphasis on applications from biology and the health sciences.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="course-materials">
    <title>Course Matrials</title>
    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Text</title>
          <p>
            <pubtitle>Introductory Statistics</pubtitle> by Hannah Seidler-Wright
          </p>
          <p>
            You can view an online copy of this free, open source textbook at <url href="https://stats.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Introductory_Statistics/Introductory_Statistics_(Hannah_Seidler-Wright)">Introductory Statistics</url>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Software</title>
          <p>
            A statistical program called R via R studio to perform statistical calculations in this course. This is free software. There will tutorial videos and lectures provided to learn this program.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Three Ring Binder</title>
          <p>
            This will be helpful to keep your notes and class worksheets in.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="online-course-comp">
    <title>Online Course Component</title>
    <p>
      In this class, you will have access to course notes, lecture videos, handouts, deadlines, and announcements on
      Canvas. If you are absent or class is canceled due to weather or other circumstances, it is your
      responsibility to log on and obtain missed notes, work, etc. Grades will be updated periodically on
      Canvas. It is also important to check your SVSU email regularly.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="course-outcomes">
    <title>Course Outcomes</title>
    <p>
      This General Education course introduces fundamental algebraic terminology and skills; demonstrates how ingenuity and creativity are essential in developing mathematical models to solve real world problems; cultivates critical thinking and logical reasoning for effective problem solving and rational decision-making in a complex technological society; and develops communication skills for translating real world situations into symbolic mathematical relationships and for interpreting mathematical results within their real world contexts.
    </p>

    <p>
      The following lists the set of outcomes covered in this course:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>Basic Probabilities</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Describing Categorical Data</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Binomial Distribution</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Normal and <m>\chi^2</m> Distributions</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Sampling Distribution for <m>\hat{p}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for One Proportion</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for Two Proportions</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for More than Two Proportions</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Describing Numerical Data</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Student <m>t</m> and <m>F</m> Distributions</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Sampling Distribution for <m>\bar{x}</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for One Mean</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for Two Means</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for More than Two Means</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Describing Two Variables</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Correlation and Linear Regression</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Inference for Correlation Coefficient</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Nonparametric Tests</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="eval">
    <title>Evaluatation</title>
    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Video Notes</title>
            <p>
              For each section covered in class, students are required to submit completed notes based on the assigned lecture videos. One submission is required per section and is due before the start of class on the day that section is covered. Notes are graded on a 0–2 scale:
            </p>

            <dl>
              <li>
                <title>2:</title>
                <p>Notes are submitted on time, are complete, and reflect engagement with the assigned videos.</p></li>
              <li>
                <title>1:</title>
                <p>Notes are submitted on time but are missing significant portions.</p></li>
              <li>
                <title>0:</title>
                <p>Notes are not submitted on time.</p></li>
            </dl>

            <p>
              Notes are graded for completion only, not for correctness. No late submissions are accepted. Notes may be handwritten, typed, or completed on a tablet, but all submissions must be uploaded as a single PDF file. Students are expected to know how to scan or create a PDF of their notes; guidance will be provided as needed.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Worksheets</title>
          <p> Worksheets are completed during class and are intended to help students practice and apply the concepts being studied. Each worksheet is paired with a MyOpenMath assignment. Students are expected to answer the questions in MyOpenMath and show supporting work on the worksheet packet. Both the completed worksheet and completion of the associated MyOpenMath assignment are required for credit. </p> 
          <p> Students may collaborate with classmates while working on worksheets and may use their notes and classroom laptops. The use of cell phones, generative AI tools, or other unauthorized resources is not permitted. Worksheets are due at the end of class and may be submitted up until the beginning of the next class period without penalty. Worksheets submitted after the start of the next class period will not be accepted. </p> 
          <p> While correctness is primarily assessed through MyOpenMath, students are expected to show clear and meaningful work on their worksheet packets. Work that is incomplete, inconsistent with the submitted answers, or does not demonstrate an attempt to solve the problem may result in reduced credit. </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Quizzes</title>
          <p> Quizzes are administered approximately every fourth class meeting throughout most of the semester. Quizzes assess a student's ability to independently apply the concepts and skills developed through class activities, worksheets, and assigned course materials. </p> 
          <p> Each quiz is scored on a 0-3 scale: </p> 
          <dl> 
            <li> 
              <title>3:</title> 
              <p>Demonstrates a strong understanding of the outcome with little to no error. Work is complete, mathematically correct, and clearly communicates the solution process.</p> 
            </li> 
            <li> 
              <title>2:</title> 
              <p>Demonstrates substantial understanding of the outcome but contains one or more minor errors. The overall solution process is appropriate and most of the work is correct.</p> 
            </li> 
            <li> 
              <title>1:</title> 
              <p>Demonstrates partial understanding of the outcome but contains significant errors or omissions. Some correct work is shown, but the student has not yet demonstrated competency.</p> 
            </li> 
            <li> 
              <title>0:</title> 
              <p>The outcome is not attempted or the work submitted does not demonstrate an understanding of the outcome.</p> 
            </li> 
          </dl> 
          <p> Each quiz includes two in-class attempts. For each quiz, the highest score earned across all attempts is used when calculating the Quiz portion of the course grade. </p> 
          <p> A third attempt is available only after attending tutoring. Students must work with a tutor to review the relevant worksheet problems and their first two attempts before a third attempt will be granted. </p> 
          <p> Quizzes are not given as make-ups. The multiple-attempt structure is intended to accommodate absences and scheduling conflicts. Students who miss an in-class attempt may use a remaining attempt to demonstrate competency. </p> 
          <p> For the final quizzes of the course, only one in-class attempt may be provided during the final exam period. Additional attempts must be completed outside of class time by appointment. </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Lab Reports</title>
          <p>
            Students will complete three lab reports during the semester. Each report
            requires students to locate an appropriate data set, analyze the data using
            the statistical methods studied in the course, and communicate their
            findings clearly. The source of the data set must be identified in the
            report.
          </p>

          <p>
            The three lab reports have the following data requirements:
            <ol marker="1.">
              <li>
                <title>Inference for Numerical Data</title>
                <p>
                  The data set must contain a quantitative response variable and a
                  categorical explanatory variable with at least three groups. There
                  must be enough observations in each group to perform an ANOVA and
                  evaluate the conditions required for the analysis.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Inference for Categorical Data</title>
                <p>
                  The data set must contain at least two categorical variables. Each
                  variable must have at least two categories so that the data can be
                  organized into a contingency table of at least size
                  <m>2\times 2</m>. There must be enough observations to perform an
                  appropriate chi-square test.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Regression</title>
                <p>
                  The data set must contain at least two quantitative variables with
                  paired observations. There must be enough observations to construct
                  and analyze a regression model.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>

          <p>
            Each lab report must contain the following components:
            <ol marker="1.">
              <li>
                <title>Data Set Summary</title>
                <p>
                  In no more than one page, describe the source and context of the data,
                  identify the variables being studied, explain how the data were
                  collected when that information is available, and state the
                  question or questions being investigated.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Graphical Analysis</title>
                <p>
                  Construct appropriate statistical graphics that help describe the
                  data and address the questions being investigated. All graphics must
                  include appropriate titles, labels, units, and legends when needed.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Statistical Analysis</title>
                <p>
                  Perform the required statistical procedures and several appropriate
                  statistical tests on the data. Include the relevant calculations,
                  verify the conditions or assumptions for each procedure, and report
                  the results using appropriate statistical notation.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Summary of Results</title>
                <p>
                  Explain the results of the statistical procedures in the context of
                  the data. The discussion should identify the important findings and
                  explain what the numerical results, confidence intervals,
                  significance tests, or models indicate.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <title>Conclusion</title>
                <p>
                  In no more than one page, summarize the principal findings, answer
                  the questions posed at the beginning of the report, and discuss any
                  important limitations of the data or analysis.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>

          <p>
            Each lab report is worth 50 points and will be evaluated using the
            following rubric.
          </p>

            <tabular
              row-headers="yes"
              halign="left"
              valign="top"
              top="medium"
              left="medium"
              bottom="medium"
              right="medium">

              <col width="30%"/>
              <col width="60%"/>
              <col width="10%"/>

              <row header="yes">
                <cell>Criterion</cell>
                <cell>Full-Credit Expectations</cell>
                <cell>Points</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Data Set Selection and Documentation</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    The data set satisfies the requirements of the assigned report, is
                    appropriate for the required analyses, and is accompanied by a
                    clearly identified source.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>2</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Data Set Summary and Research Questions</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    The summary clearly explains the context, variables, collection
                    method when available, and questions being investigated. The summary
                    is no longer than one page.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>4</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Graphical Analysis</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    Appropriate graphics are constructed correctly and support the
                    analysis. Graphics are readable and contain suitable titles, labels,
                    units, and legends.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Statistical Methods and Computations</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    The required procedures and tests are selected and performed
                    correctly. Relevant calculations are included, statistical notation
                    is used appropriately, and required conditions or assumptions are
                    addressed.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Results and Interpretation</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    Results are reported accurately and interpreted in the context of
                    the data. The discussion explains the meaning of the statistical
                    findings rather than merely listing software output or numerical
                    results.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Conclusion</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    The conclusion answers the questions being investigated, summarizes
                    the principal findings, and discusses important limitations. The
                    conclusion is no longer than one page.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>5</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Organization and Presentation</cell>
                <cell>
                  <p>
                    The report is clearly organized, professionally presented, and
                    written in complete sentences. Tables, graphics, calculations, and
                    statistical output are placed logically and referenced in the text.
                  </p>
                </cell>
                <cell>4</cell>
              </row>

              <row>
                <cell>Total</cell>
                <cell></cell>
                <cell>30</cell>
              </row>

  </tabular>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="grade">

    <title>Grading</title>

    <p>
      Your final course grade will be determined by your performance in four
      categories: Video Notes, Worksheets, Quizzes, and Lab Reports. A letter
      grade will be determined separately for each category using the
      requirements below. Your final course grade will be the lowest of the four
      category grades.
    </p>
    <tabular
    row-headers="yes"
    halign="left"
    valign="top"
    top="medium"
    left="medium"
    bottom="medium"
    right="medium">

    <col width="12%"/>
    <col width="22%"/>
    <col width="22%"/>
    <col width="20%"/>
    <col width="24%"/>

    <row header="yes">
      <cell>Letter Grade</cell>
      <cell>Video Notes Completed</cell>
      <cell>Worksheets Completed</cell>
      <cell>Quiz Points</cell>
      <cell>Lab Report Points</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>A</cell>
      <cell>90%–100%</cell>
      <cell>90%–100%</cell>
      <cell>40-45</cell>
      <cell>77-90</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>B</cell>
      <cell>80% to less than 90%</cell>
      <cell>80% to less than 90%</cell>
      <cell>33-39</cell>
      <cell>63-76</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>C</cell>
      <cell>70% to less than 80%</cell>
      <cell>70% to less than 80%</cell>
      <cell>27-32</cell>
      <cell>50-62</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>D</cell>
      <cell>60% to less than 70%</cell>
      <cell>60% to less than 70%</cell>
      <cell>18-26</cell>
      <cell>36-49</cell>
    </row>

    <row>
      <cell>F</cell>
      <cell>Less than 60%</cell>
      <cell>Less than 60%</cell>
      <cell>0-17</cell>
      <cell>0-35</cell>
    </row>

  </tabular>

    <p>
      Here are some examples showing how final course grades are determined:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          A student completes 94% of the Video Notes, completes 92% of the
          Worksheets, earns 56 Quiz points, and earns 140 Lab Report points.
          The student earns an A in all four categories. Therefore, the
          student's final course grade is an A.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          A student completes 95% of the Video Notes, completes 84% of the
          Worksheets, earns 55 Quiz points, and earns 135 Lab Report points.
          The student earns an A for Video Notes, a B for Worksheets, an A for
          Quizzes, and an A for Lab Reports. Since the lowest category grade is
          a B, the student's final course grade is a B.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          A student completes 83% of the Video Notes, completes 84% of the
          Worksheets, earns 51 Quiz points, and earns 100 Lab Report points.
          The student earns a B for Video Notes, a B for Worksheets, a B for
          Quizzes, and a C for Lab Reports. Since the lowest category grade is
          a C, the student's final course grade is a C.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    
    <p>
      Plus and minus grades may be assigned based on a student's overall
      participation and effort throughout the semester, including class
      participation and attendance at office hours or tutoring sessions. For
      example, a student whose category grade is a B but who demonstrates limited
      participation and effort may receive a B-, while a student whose category
      grade is a C and who demonstrates a high level of participation and effort
      may receive a C+. A student who demonstrates the expected level of
      participation and effort will receive the unmodified letter grade. SVSU does
      not award C-, D+, or D- grades, so these grades will not be assigned.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="tips">
    <title>Tips for Success</title>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read the textbook before attending class. The instructors explanations will make more sense and when they don't you will be more likely able to ask a relevant question.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Attend each class session, both physically and mentally.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read the syllabus and look over the schedule on the last page of the syllabus.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Build community with other students in the course and create study groups.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Work outside of class. Math is learned by doing not seeing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Ask for help. Stop in to see me, email me, call me, or ask others in class for help.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For free math tutoring; find information about the Math &amp; Physics Learning Center at <url href="https://www.svsu.edu/mathandphysicslearningcenter/">MPLC</url>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="academic-integrity">
    <title>Academic Integrity Policy</title>

    <p>
      In MATH 132B, any occurrence of cheating or academic dishonesty may be
      officially documented. The student or students involved may then receive a
      copy of the documentation. The first occurrence may result in failure of
      the coursework and will be reported to the Office of Student Conduct
      Programs for the record. Any additional occurrence will be reported to the
      Office of Student Conduct Programs for adjudication.
    </p>

    <p>
      You may work in groups or with tutors on homework and lab reports when
      permitted, but you must complete your own work. Quizzes must be completed
      independently without resources. Inappropriate use of laptops, cell phones,
      calculators, tablets, websites, outside assistance, or other resources is
      considered cheating. If you are unsure whether something is permitted, ask
      your instructor before using it.
    </p>

    <p>
      See
      <url href="https://www.svsu.edu/studentconductprograms/academicintegrity/academicintegritypolicy/">
        SVSU's Academic Integrity Policy
      </url>
      for additional information.
    </p>
  </subsection>

